Hi Andrew,

You make a fair point regarding the threat model. I agree that requiring host-level control to inject malicious emulated PCI devices makes this impractical as a real-world exploit. 

To give some context on why this was triggered: we are not targeting the Hamachi device specifically. We are currently running a broad, automated fuzzing campaign across all PCI/PCIe drivers in the kernel tree to stress-test the driver core and error-handling paths. Hamachi was tested simply because it gets built in generic configurations like `allyesconfig`.

That being said, I completely agree with your suggestion. If the hardware is long obsolete, removing the driver is the most pragmatic solution. It eliminates dead code, drops the maintenance burden, and naturally resolves this fuzzing roadblock.

Would you prefer I submit a patch that entirely removes `drivers/net/ethernet/packetengines/hamachi.c` and its associated Kconfig/Makefile entries, or will the maintainers take it from here?

Best regards,
Mingyu Wang


